solution.p:
We  multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate radical:  
\begin{array}{rcl}  \displaystyle \lim_{ x \rightarrow \infty } 
\left( \sqrt{ x^2 + 10} - \sqrt{x^2 - 4} \right) 
\amp =\amp   \displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty } 
\left( \sqrt{x^2 + 10} - \sqrt{x^2 - 4 } \right)    
\frac{\sqrt{x^2 + 10} + \sqrt{x^2-4}}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 10} + \sqrt{x^2- 4}}
\\ 
\amp =\amp   \displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty }  \frac{(x^2 + 10) -  (x^2-4)}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 10} + \sqrt{x^2 - 4}}
\\  \amp  = \amp  \displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty }  \frac{ 14}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 10} + \sqrt{x^2 - 4 }} 
\end{array} 
Notice that the denominator of this last expression 
\left(\sqrt{ x^2 + 10} + \sqrt{x^2 - 4 } \right) becomes large as 
x \to \infty (it's bigger than x). 
So 
\lim_{ x \rightarrow \infty } 
\left( \sqrt{ x^2 + 10} - \sqrt{x^2 - 4} \right)  =
\displaystyle \lim_{ x \to  \infty }  \frac{ 14}{\sqrt{ x^2 + 10} + \sqrt{x^2 - 4 }}    = 0
